86049816 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049816 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049816 #edb/ref 1031 "Sketching a Function From the Graph of its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049816 #edb/ref 1033 1206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049816 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "16166227-96e3-5281-9560-ed98cac88477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175582 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b944462a-0d9c-56d1-ba28-a212c5ca0888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175765 #edb/ref 1069 "e-338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175765 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph $y=g'(x),$ the derivative of $y=g(x),$ is shown below. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $g(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1.5$\n\nII. $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\nIII. $g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=3.5$\n\n![](images/58/582d792bf3854b0060e23e171fdc0c72e6486d09b0f1be47552ec4338d3f8ba1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175765 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "403f94fd-793b-55eb-a7c4-14f97cf4ca79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175765 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $y=g'(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbfcc5d729dea273d9b60c2ef80e10696aa0631698a7cb4ff009f54fb59b1ea3.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Checking the first derivative at the points $A$ and $B,$ we see that\n\n- the point $A,$ at $x=1.5,$ is a relative maximum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from positive to negative, and\n\n- the point $B,$ at $x=5,$ is a relative minimum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y=g''(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ is horizontal) or $y=g''(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). Indeed, at the point $x=3.5,$ which we label as $C,$ we see that the slope of $g'(x)$ is zero, meaning that $g''(x)=0.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4e945f8e45cd67982246dbd1798f3958eff114f7dc04ff98c3a30f5f570eff9.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from negative to positive. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, all three statements are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175796 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175796 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-2,3]$ as shown below. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in(-2,3).$ \n\n![](images/1d/1da07563588809f268054c88f684f47c701f4daeeb2bfdcb5c01d3f4d9aaa397.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a25d851-2f79-5e70-bde2-eda99697fe3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175796 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $f'(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of $f''(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is increasing, we have $f''(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f''(x)<       0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $f''(x)=0$ or $f''(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- $f''(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f''(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a point of inflection of $y=f(x).$\n\nNote that the sign of $f''(x)$ does not change at $x=-1,$ and so this is *not* a point of inflection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175900 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175947 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1045 "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Concavity and Points of Inflection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176100 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a54a0eb8-1c10-55bc-94b1-373798ccde02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/199a0809c86f57743fe954d1221656198f92339f146722928b46e598680d299f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b96e246-6e1a-5cb7-9888-3f16ec267c72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176574 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176603 #edb/ref 1061 1241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f8c6b188-b62b-5303-8c70-a1b3b79fe9c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176604 #edb/ref 1061 1242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f16dbb41-7982-5668-b0bb-22d6b038dbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1061 25381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71c365fe-68f2-5852-89cf-41fb104a5949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that we can use the second derivative to determine the concavity of a differentiable function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) > 0$ on $(a,b),$ while\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) < 0$ on $(a,b).$\n\nSo, to determine intervals of concavity of $f(x)$ by looking at the graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is increasing, then $f''(x)>0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](images/8a/8ac780a2dfa4d6c32449b750ff236568764de920d38e363d1badf4c7324c9c94.png)\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is decreasing, then $f''(x)<0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcc5e92916f582dc75450241c0223b55dab287d24e06b55e4d8fde56c7f927c.png)\n\nAlso, recall that $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if $f''(x)$ changes its sign around $x=a.$ So, to determine the inflection points of $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa). \n\nIn other words, we need to pay attention to relative extrema on the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/68/68a0a5523690eceb319bff14916528b6c1727d68e393373553bc42fbf6be6f16.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1065 649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8ef5e0fb-85b8-5d0c-890a-85fa61aaef1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516114 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=\\pm 1$ and $x=\\pm 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516114 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516114 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0139d7bf-fb60-5bb3-895a-29c8b2baffc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516129 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum and two inflection points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516129 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516129 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09f70fff-74e5-5199-bd4b-3d36c38c70d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516150 #edb/ref 1080 "$g(x)$ has on