2186516751 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516751 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516751 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516751 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516751 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dbc98646-cfbc-5ee7-82cb-9e03b52e88b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516756 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516756 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516756 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516756 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e99ddf10-2ad1-5e8d-b98e-05b3db34966c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/1505bc3714405515ec7d5a834c6d08f849b3adbf8678a76b3b5cf27ccf394e28.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. In **ascending order**, the points of inflection of  $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x=$\n\n{{field-2}}, $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "172429bc-7db9-5917-9fbf-cfae2b8af5b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516776 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2$ (note that ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=-1$ and $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the points of inflection are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5a/5a753c70df3b523ccdeead554bee653ebba355d41ef95aea7ace17236fd38829.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=g(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cc6f806-f012-5062-b71f-ffac34d675d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516781 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $y={g}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A$ and $B$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42e073c044b6bf9ced663bcb55bbd89d7ac79f85f918ca3364d2be3b2f8e74bc.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have an extremum at $B$ only. This is a relative maximum since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are points the $A$, $C$ and $D$ on the graph.\n\n![](images/65/650e68ff0dfbb6f0b679559a46532f2e29fb970f392a6bd4932b8363c2aa6777.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $A$, where the slope goes from negative to positive, and an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from positive to negative.\n\nTherefore, we get one relative maximum and two inflection points on $[-4,4]$ for $y=g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6d/6d9d6bbd1f54319a6bf1aa8dae03d6ea7dc19e8430e7a5bfd72e805edbcfb6d5.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-1,1)$\n\nII. $g(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "613b77ce-0261-57ef-a0c0-d6ed0660c89d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516807 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph ${g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(0,1).$ Hence, ${g}^{″}(x)$ must be positive on $(0,1)$ and therefore, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)⊂(-1,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can conclude that ${g}^{′}(-2)$ exists and that it is not equal to $0.$ So, $x=-2$ is not a critical point of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the first derivative ${g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1,$ from negative to positive. Therefore $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/30e2b42fd5b3ab5fb1ee538079b518fa4b98a637e81d7f9aad23b4dd718a4541.png)\n\nThe graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=h(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. The graph of $y=h(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-2,1)$\n\nIII. $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "627aa48c-c5a6-5e2c-bbad-89df3a735c3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516808 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is true. The graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at $x=-2$ meaning that ${h}^{′}(x)=0.$ Moreover, ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign by going from positive to negative. Thus, there is a relative maximum at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The slope of the tangent line to $y={h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign somewhere near $x=0.$ Hence, the graph $y=h(x)$ has an inflection point inside $(-2,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that ${h}^{′}(3)=0$ but ${h}^{′}(x)$ does not change its sign around this point. Thus, there is no extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19ebd6ce74bf6b25b920869403effa677c6a4bef2dedef38a81f77794c99a195.png)\n\nThe graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=h(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-3$\n\nII. $h(x)$ has an inflection point on the interval $(0,2)$\n\nIII. $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90895ef1-1562-5ad7-aeaa-a3c532c8d41b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516826 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ does not change sign around $x=-3$, therefore, there cannot be a relative extremum at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The slope of the tangent line to $y={h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign somewhere near $x=1.$ Hence, the graph $y=h(x)$ has an inflection point inside $(0,2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ does not change sign around $x=2$, therefore, there cannot be a relative extremum at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9ffebb1757b03211d1dd7d8d53ea8a02bd5262534fb868b44662f5a4e5f6fbd8.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa64cd03-641d-5fbe-b490-fd0f93407035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516833 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-1$ and $x=0$ (note that ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=1$). Therefore, $x=-1$ and $x=0$ are  the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e9da73fadd389e8cb47c58eb980e0f2dee6b36f3bb0475466090bd5ac3bfb35c.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-4,4]$ and shown above. Find all the inflection points for $y=f(x)$ on $(-4,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b39bf78a-2c45-5cec-8ca0-7f6b069d4c47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516837 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point of the interval $x\\in [-4,4].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-4,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $\\text{const.}$ |  | $↗$ | $0$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $0$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only  at the point $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is the only point of inflection of $y=f(x).$\n\n**Watch out!** The second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=-1$ or $x=1.$  Hence, neither $x=-1$ nor $x=1$ are points of inflection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e54c40efd2e67f41ab726eaf3ef13c56aab8acb56222e8db3c53de47e6bcf547.png)\n\nThe graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b66a1e23-ff78-5796-afef-31e3ad188600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516841 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $y={f}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76c04c26b2641990e4528fb3e89c4ef72c7a27c67e289c88cb1d6034baf67ef7.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=f(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have a relative minimum at $A,$  since the derivative goes from negative to positive, and a relative maximum at $B,$  since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={f}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are the points $C$, $D,$ $E,$ and $F$ as shown on the graph below.\n\n![](images/12/1264de8104784e808891fbe44b176e0624e05fa1ea8058cf533c732016eca63d.png)\n\n \n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have inflection points only at $C,D,$ and $E.$ Indeed, at points $C$ and $E$ the slope goes from positive to negative, while at $D$ the slope goes from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, we get two relative extrema and three inflection points on $[-2,5]$ for $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a1/a16176b3fe7ff15ca90aef4a829d53d5f8e64c16c9e0f623ca0d7378cc7fa8f1.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4806679-d98f-5bc2-87be-0a6d9963a840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516857 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$ \n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-2,$  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=-2,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are  the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
